\uc0\u55357 \u56538  Book Review: In Plain Sight by Richard DeVeau\
\
I thought I knew what kind of thriller this would be, but about twenty pages in I realized I was clearing my evening to finish it.\
\
In Plain Sight picks up a year after the Greater Boston Massacre, and the weight of that event hangs over everything Eve Tuant does. She\'92s sharper here, more guarded, and honestly more exhausted\'97and that made her feel real. The shift from the chaos of the first book into the snake pit of Washington politics was handled really well. I liked how the story leaned into backroom deals, carefully worded conversations, and the constant sense that someone is always listening. More than once I caught myself rereading a scene because I wasn\'92t sure who was playing whom, which felt very intentional.\
\
A couple things stood out. The idea that three conspirators are still embedded at the highest levels of government adds a steady undercurrent of dread, and the looming assassination plot keeps the tension tight without turning the book into nonstop action. There\'92s also a stretch where Eve realizes she\'92s been marked for elimination, and from that point on the paranoia ramps up fast\'97in a good way. It reminded me a bit of early Vince Flynn or Brad Meltzer, but with a more personal, grounded lead.\
\
If I\'92m nitpicking, there were a few political maneuvering sections that slowed the pace just slightly. I didn\'92t mind them, but readers looking for constant action might notice the dip. For me, the payoff was worth it.\
\
I finished this yesterday and immediately wanted book three. If you like smart political thrillers, high-stakes conspiracies, and a capable female protagonist who doesn\'92t feel invincible, I\'92d absolutely recommend In Plain Sight. It\'92s a strong continuation and a step up in confidence from book one.}